{
  "_provenance": {
    "captured": "2026-08-05",
    "why": "A Manifold PSEUDO_NUMERIC market's `probability` is the traded value's normalized POSITION on the market's [min, max] scale, not a probability, and the market carries NO `answers` array -- so the array-presence discriminator read it as BINARY and the rendered `prob` column printed 0.48 on a market whose actual estimate is ~121 years. Captured live because the fix rests on two facts a hand-built fixture could not establish: the SEARCH listing already carries value/min/max/isLogScale (so no extra request is needed), and `value` is the only trustworthy source for the number (see log_scale below).",
    "endpoints": {
      "search_pseudo_numeric": "https://api.manifold.markets/v0/search-markets?term=age+of+the+oldest+person+alive+in+2100&contractType=ALL&limit=5",
      "search_log_scale": "https://api.manifold.markets/v0/search-markets?term=&contractType=PSEUDO_NUMERIC&limit=25&sort=liquidity",
      "search_stonk": "https://api.manifold.markets/v0/search-markets?term=&contractType=STONK&limit=25&sort=liquidity",
      "search_scale_extremes": "https://api.manifold.markets/v0/search-markets?term=population&contractType=PSEUDO_NUMERIC&limit=30"
    },
    "edits": "String values over 300 chars truncated. No key renamed, added or removed and NO NUMERIC VALUE TOUCHED -- every assertion in the suite is arithmetic over these figures, so a rounded one would make the fixture agree with a wrong parser. search_log_scale keeps the 2 isLogScale rows of that 25-row listing; search_stonk keeps 1 of 25 (they are identical in shape). search_scale_extremes keeps 4 of that listing's 30 rows, chosen for scale shape, numerics untouched.",
    "payloads": {
      "search_pseudo_numeric": "baZgu5Ttuukkeh6DIWCD, the market that exposed the bug -- it was ranked `same_quantity_same_date` for Metaculus Q14333 ('Age of oldest human as of 2100'), i.e. the single most relevant market on the slate, and rendered `prob` 0.48. probability * max == value to full float precision (0.48386766931955777 * 250 == 120.96691732988944), which is the receipt that the field is a scale position. isLogScale false, so this is the LINEAR case.",
      "search_log_scale": "Two live isLogScale markets, the reason nothing may ever RECOMPUTE the value from `probability`: on min=2000/max=250000 the API's own value is 5817 where probability*max is 166939 (29x), and on min=1/max=10000000 it is 609 where probability*max is 3991142 (6554x). A log interpolation between the bounds does not reproduce it either (50271 and 942), so the mapping is Manifold's own and `value` is the only honest source.",
      "search_stonk": "9iyWFCawV7RBXiGIdFRz, a STONK market -- the other Manifold type with no `answers` array. Present as the allow-list's control: it publishes probability=null AND no value/min/max (verified on its detail payload too), so it is priceless on the search alone and renders `-` under any design. It is the reason the price read is an allow-list on BINARY rather than a PSEUDO_NUMERIC deny-list: this is a second scalar-ish type that already looks BINARY to the array discriminator.",
      "search_scale_extremes": "Four rows of that 30-row listing, each pinning a case the other payloads cannot. (1) The 2040 human-population market, isLogScale with min=1e6/max=2e10: its API value is 7.85e9 where the naive linear map min+prob*(max-min) gives 1.92e10, so a recompute would show a forecaster twice the world's population. (2) An isLogScale market whose value is exactly 1 against a naive 389585 -- the extreme of the same hazard, 389585x. (3) A LINEAR market on min=0/max=1, whose traded value 0.468 is shaped exactly like a probability: the case where magnitude cannot tell a forecaster which kind of number they are reading, and the reason the rendered cell carries a `value` label rather than relying on the number looking large. (4) A linear percentage market, second witness that the naive map reproduces `value` EXACTLY on linear markets -- measured to 2.2e-16 across all 11 linear markets in the listing, which is precisely why a recompute-based implementation passes every linear test and fails only in the wild."
    }
  },
